IMPORT prelude

-- Secretary of the Synod of the Diocese of Singapore Ordinance 1959 (Singapore) -- who the Corporation "the Secretary of the Synod of
-- the Diocese of Singapore" is and how a successor Secretary qualifies (ss 2(1), 4), what the Corporation may do with property and in
-- court (ss 2(3), 2(4), 5(2)), how its seal is used and what a signature proves (s 3), which lands the Ordinance moved from the Bishop
-- of Singapore to the Corporation (s 5 and the Schedule), and whose rights are saved (s 6).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/SSDSO1959.txt. The deposit says the edition
-- "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021". The only amendment
-- annotation in the body is "[7/97]" under s 6. The Ordinance is dated 26 March 1959.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0250 in ../../../requirements.jsonl: tier 4 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by everyday-life
-- relevance. It asks what the Ordinance decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a sharper question yet.
-- The Ordinance is six sections and a Schedule, so all of it is in scope. For a person outside the Synod it matters when buying,
-- leasing or lending against land held by the Corporation (ss 2(3), 3), when suing it or being sued by it (s 2(4)), and for title to
-- the twenty church sites listed in the Schedule (s 5).
--
-- NOT ENCODED: s 1 (short title); the recitals (they record facts, they decide nothing); the content of s 48 of the Conveyancing and
-- Law of Property Act 1886, to which s 3(2) refers (whether a power of attorney was "deposited" under it is a stated fact); the Bishop of
-- Singapore Ordinance 1911, under which the scheduled lands were held before (see that subject's own row); the lot numbers and areas of
-- the Schedule, beyond the estate in each item and the term of its two leases; the Synod's own rules for appointing a Secretary.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Secretary of the Synod of the Diocese of Singapore Ordinance 1959 -- the Corporation`

§§ `2(1) and 4 -- who is the Corporation, and how a successor Secretary qualifies`

-- s 2(1): "The said Roland Keith Hudson and his successors from time to time in the office of the Secretary of the Synod of the Diocese
-- of Singapore duly qualified as hereinafter provided shall be a body corporate (hereinafter called 'the Corporation')".
-- s 4(1): "No successor of the said Roland Keith Hudson in the office of Secretary shall be deemed to be duly qualified as aforesaid
-- unless and until he has caused an extract from the Minutes of the Synod appointing him such Secretary certified as correct by the
-- Bishop of Singapore or in his absence a Commissary appointed by the Bishop or the Archbishop of Canterbury during a vacancy in the See
-- of Singapore to be published in the Gazette."
-- s 4(2): "Such publication shall be sufficient evidence of the appointment, and that the person named therein is duly qualified".
--
-- Three things must be true of a successor: appointment by the Synod (recorded in its Minutes), certification of the extract by the
-- right person, and publication of the extract in the Gazette. Until then he is not "duly qualified", and so on the words of s 2(1) not
-- the Corporation. Hudson himself is named in s 2(1) and needs no publication.
--
-- WHO MAY CERTIFY. The clause "a Commissary appointed by the Bishop or the Archbishop of Canterbury during a vacancy in the See" can be
-- read two ways. Encoded reading: a Commissary appointed by the Bishop (when the Bishop is absent) or a Commissary appointed by the
-- Archbishop of Canterbury (when the See is vacant). The other reading lets the Archbishop certify in person during a vacancy; it is
-- not encoded, and the Archbishop certifying in person is treated as not satisfying s 4(1). This choice is an inference from the
-- grammar, not something the text settles.
DECLARE Certifier IS ONE OF
    `the Bishop of Singapore`
    `a Commissary appointed by the Bishop`
    `a Commissary appointed by the Archbishop of Canterbury`
    `the Archbishop of Canterbury in person`
    `someone else`

DECLARE Situation IS ONE OF
    `the Bishop is present`
    `the Bishop is absent`
    `the See of Singapore is vacant`

DECLARE Claimant HAS
    `is Roland Keith Hudson` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is appointed Secretary in the Minutes of the Synod` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the extract is certified by` IS A Certifier
    `the situation at certification` IS A Situation
    `the certified extract is published in the Gazette` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the certifier` IS A Certifier, `the situation` IS A Situation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `may certify the extract` IF
    CONSIDER `the certifier`
    WHEN `the Bishop of Singapore` THEN NOT (`the situation` EQUALS `the See of Singapore is vacant`)
    WHEN `a Commissary appointed by the Bishop` THEN `the situation` EQUALS `the Bishop is absent`
    WHEN `a Commissary appointed by the Archbishop of Canterbury` THEN `the situation` EQUALS `the See of Singapore is vacant`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the claimant` IS A Claimant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a duly qualified successor` IF
        `the claimant`'s `is appointed Secretary in the Minutes of the Synod`
    AND `may certify the extract` (`the claimant`'s `the extract is certified by`) (`the claimant`'s `the situation at certification`)
    AND `the claimant`'s `the certified extract is published in the Gazette`

GIVEN `the claimant` IS A Claimant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is the Corporation` IF
        `the claimant`'s `is Roland Keith Hudson`
    OR  `is a duly qualified successor` `the claimant`

-- s 4(2): publication is "sufficient evidence" of the appointment and of due qualification. It turns on publication alone.
GIVEN `the claimant` IS A Claimant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `publication is sufficient evidence of qualification` IF
    `the claimant`'s `the certified extract is published in the Gazette`

§§ `2(2) to 2(4) and 5(2) -- what the Ordinance empowers the Corporation to do`

-- s 2(2): "The said seal may from time to time be broken, changed, altered and made anew as the Corporation thinks fit."
-- s 2(3): "The Corporation may acquire, purchase, take, hold and enjoy movable and immovable property of every description and may sell,
-- convey, assign, surrender and yield up, mortgage, demise, re-assign, transfer or otherwise dispose of any movable and immovable
-- property vested in the Corporation upon such terms as to the Standing Committee of the Synod of the Diocese of Singapore seem fit,
-- and the minutes of the Standing Committee shall be sufficient evidence of such terms."
-- s 2(4): "The Corporation may sue or be sued in respect of its property and otherwise in all courts of justice."
-- s 5(2): the Corporation "shall have full powers as such trustee to deal with the said property as provided in section 2".
--
-- Disposal reaches only property "vested in the Corporation", and on terms that seem fit to the Standing Committee -- not to the
-- Corporation. Reading "upon such terms as to the Standing Committee seem fit" as requiring the Standing Committee to have approved the
-- terms is an inference from the words. Unlike the Bishop of Singapore Ordinance 1911, the capacity to sue and be sued is not confined
-- to property: s 2(4) adds "and otherwise".
DECLARE Power IS ONE OF
    `break and remake its corporate seal`
    `acquire or purchase property`
    `hold and enjoy property`
    `sell property`
    `mortgage property`
    `lease property`
    `transfer or otherwise dispose of property`
    `sue in respect of its property`
    `be sued in respect of its property`
    `sue on a matter not about its property`
    `be sued on a matter not about its property`
    `borrow money without security`

GIVEN `the power` IS A Power, `the property is vested in the Corporation` IS A BOOLEAN, `the Standing Committee approved the terms` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Ordinance empowers the Corporation to` IF
    CONSIDER `the power`
    WHEN `break and remake its corporate seal` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `acquire or purchase property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `hold and enjoy property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `sell property` THEN `the property is vested in the Corporation` AND `the Standing Committee approved the terms`
    WHEN `mortgage property` THEN `the property is vested in the Corporation` AND `the Standing Committee approved the terms`
    WHEN `lease property` THEN `the property is vested in the Corporation` AND `the Standing Committee approved the terms`
    WHEN `transfer or otherwise dispose of property` THEN `the property is vested in the Corporation` AND `the Standing Committee approved the terms`
    WHEN `sue in respect of its property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `be sued in respect of its property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `sue on a matter not about its property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `be sued on a matter not about its property` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `3 -- using the seal, and what the signatures prove`

-- s 3(1): "The seal of the Corporation shall not be fixed to any deed, document or other instrument except by the authority of a
-- Resolution of the Standing Committee of the Synod of the Diocese of Singapore, and in the presence of a member of the Standing
-- Committee duly authorised as witness in that resolution."
-- s 3(2): instruments "shall be sealed with the seal of the Corporation in the presence of the said Roland Keith Hudson or his attorney,
-- duly authorised by a power of attorney deposited under section 48 of the Conveyancing and Law of Property Act 1886 or in the presence
-- of his successor for the time being in the office of Secretary ... or his attorney duly authorised as aforesaid and shall also be
-- signed by the said Roland Keith Hudson or his attorney or his said successor or his said attorney and the member of the Standing
-- Committee duly authorised as witness."
-- s 3(3): "Such signing shall be sufficient evidence that the said seal was duly and properly affixed and the said seal is the lawful
-- seal of the Corporation."
--
-- Six things in all: the seal; a Standing Committee resolution authorising it; the presence of the member that resolution names as
-- witness; the presence of the Secretary or a deposited attorney; the signature of the Secretary or a deposited attorney; and the
-- signature of the authorised witness. s 3(3) speaks of "such signing": read here as both signatures required by s 3(2) on a sealed
-- instrument (an inference from "such"). Whether a resolution was passed is not part of what the signing proves.
DECLARE Officer IS ONE OF
    `the Secretary for the time being`
    `an attorney under a deposited power of attorney`
    `an attorney whose power of attorney is not deposited`
    `a church officer with no power of attorney`
    `nobody`

DECLARE Instrument HAS
    `is sealed with the Corporation seal` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a Standing Committee resolution authorises the sealing` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the witness named in the resolution is present` IS A BOOLEAN
    `sealed in the presence of` IS AN Officer
    `signed by` IS AN Officer
    `is signed by the witness named in the resolution` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the officer` IS AN Officer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is the Secretary or a deposited attorney` IF
    CONSIDER `the officer`
    WHEN `the Secretary for the time being` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an attorney under a deposited power of attorney` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the instrument` IS AN Instrument
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is executed as section 3 requires` IF
        `the instrument`'s `is sealed with the Corporation seal`
    AND `the instrument`'s `a Standing Committee resolution authorises the sealing`
    AND `the instrument`'s `the witness named in the resolution is present`
    AND `is the Secretary or a deposited attorney` (`the instrument`'s `sealed in the presence of`)
    AND `is the Secretary or a deposited attorney` (`the instrument`'s `signed by`)
    AND `the instrument`'s `is signed by the witness named in the resolution`

GIVEN `the instrument` IS AN Instrument
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the signing is sufficient evidence the seal was duly affixed` IF
        `the instrument`'s `is sealed with the Corporation seal`
    AND `is the Secretary or a deposited attorney` (`the instrument`'s `signed by`)
    AND `the instrument`'s `is signed by the witness named in the resolution`

§§ `5 and the Schedule -- the lands moved from the Bishop to the Corporation`

-- s 5(1): "The Bishop of Singapore incorporated in whom are vested the lands hereditaments and premises specified in the Schedule ... is
-- hereby divested of such lands hereditaments and premises and all estates rights and interests therein."
-- s 5(2): "All lands hereditaments and premises specified in the Schedule ... are hereby vested in the Corporation as trustee for the
-- Synod of the Diocese of Singapore for the respective estates, rights and interests for which the same are held".
-- The Schedule is headed "Lands in Singapore vested in the Bishop of Singapore incorporated under the Bishop of Singapore Ordinance
-- 1911 prior to 26 March 1959" and has twenty items.
--
-- Only land in the Schedule moves; land the Bishop held that is not scheduled stays with the Bishop. The Corporation takes it as
-- trustee for the Synod, for the same estate the Bishop had -- the Ordinance does not enlarge a leasehold into a freehold.
DECLARE Holding HAS
    `is specified in the Schedule` IS A BOOLEAN
    `was vested in the Bishop of Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the holding` IS A Holding
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 5 vests it in the Corporation as trustee for the Synod` IF
    `the holding`'s `is specified in the Schedule`

GIVEN `the holding` IS A Holding
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 5 divests the Bishop of it` IF
        `the holding`'s `is specified in the Schedule`
    AND `the holding`'s `was vested in the Bishop of Singapore`

-- The "Estate" column of the Schedule, item by item, as printed. The PDF table is column-shifted in the deposit; the estate is matched
-- to the item by order. Item 1 (St. Andrew's Cathedral) has two lots on two estates: lot 20-3 "Statutory Land Grant No. 33925" and lot
-- 20-4 "Leasehold No. 5073 for a term of 999 years from 10 November 1951". Statutory land grants are items 1 (in part), 5, 9 and 10;
-- item 11 is "Leasehold No. 4841 for a term of 30 years from 1-1-1947"; item 20 is "Pt. Grant No. 67", which says neither freehold nor
-- leasehold; items 2-4, 6-8 and 12-19 are freehold grants.
DECLARE Estate IS ONE OF
    `statutory land grant and leasehold`
    `statutory land grant`
    `freehold grant`
    `leasehold`
    `part of a grant`
    `not an item of the Schedule`

GIVEN `item number` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is an item of the Schedule` IF
    `item number` AT LEAST 1 AND `item number` AT MOST 20

GIVEN `item number` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH AN Estate
DECIDE `the estate in Schedule item` IS
    IF NOT `is an item of the Schedule` `item number` THEN `not an item of the Schedule`
    ELSE IF `item number` EQUALS 1 THEN `statutory land grant and leasehold`
        ELSE IF `item number` EQUALS 11 THEN `leasehold`
            ELSE IF `item number` EQUALS 20 THEN `part of a grant`
                ELSE IF `item number` EQUALS 5 OR `item number` EQUALS 9 OR `item number` EQUALS 10 THEN `statutory land grant`
                    ELSE `freehold grant`

-- The two leases in the Schedule, and whether each is still running in a given year on its own printed term. Years only: the
-- 999-year lease from 10 November 1951 runs to 10 November 2950, and the 30-year lease from 1 January 1947 to the end of 1976. That
-- a lease held "for the respective estates" ends when its term ends is general law, not a statement in the Ordinance; the arithmetic is
-- from the Schedule's own figures.
DECLARE ScheduledLease IS ONE OF
    `the 999-year lease of T.S. 10 lot 20-4`
    `the 30-year lease of T.S. 23 lot 145-12`

GIVEN `the lease` IS A ScheduledLease
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the lease starts in year` IS
    CONSIDER `the lease`
    WHEN `the 999-year lease of T.S. 10 lot 20-4` THEN 1951
    WHEN `the 30-year lease of T.S. 23 lot 145-12` THEN 1947

GIVEN `the lease` IS A ScheduledLease
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the lease term in years` IS
    CONSIDER `the lease`
    WHEN `the 999-year lease of T.S. 10 lot 20-4` THEN 999
    WHEN `the 30-year lease of T.S. 23 lot 145-12` THEN 30

GIVEN `the lease` IS A ScheduledLease
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the lease ends in year` IS
    `the lease starts in year` `the lease` PLUS `the lease term in years` `the lease`

-- Strictly after the start year and before the end year, so the two boundary years (part-year either side) are never asked.
GIVEN `the lease` IS A ScheduledLease, `the year` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the lease is running throughout year` IF
        `the year` GREATER THAN `the lease starts in year` `the lease`
    AND `the year` LESS THAN `the lease ends in year` `the lease`

§§ `6 -- whose rights are saved`

-- s 6: "Nothing in this Ordinance shall affect the rights of the Government, all bodies politic and corporate, and all others except
-- such as are mentioned in this Ordinance and those claiming by, from or under them."
GIVEN `is mentioned in the Ordinance` IS A BOOLEAN, `claims by, from or under a person mentioned` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Ordinance may affect their rights` IF
    `is mentioned in the Ordinance` OR `claims by, from or under a person mentioned`
